the grid-bound nest is one context of 4 blocks of 64 threads holding all its operations: true
the serial-batch twin is one context of 2 blocks: its batch loop adds work, not blocks: true
4 blocks of 64 threads fill P=4 at t=64; 2 blocks fill half: true
without a fill model the twins tie on the compute leg: true
with it the serial-batch twin's compute leg is exactly twice the grid-bound one's: true
a fill model under which every context is full scores bitwise what no fill model does: true
the zero nest contributes no context: only the contraction's 1 block of 64 threads: true
so the kernel's fill is the contraction's quarter, though its launch spans 16 blocks: true
2 blocks of 128 threads fill half of P=4 at t=64 (not all of it: a block is on one multiprocessor): true
8 blocks of 128 threads fill it: true
two nests of equal work, at fills 1 and 1/4, price as 1 + 4 times one nest's work: true
the tile-MMA statement is one tile context of 1 block: true
accepted tile-MMA work prices at the ceiling times its context's fill: true
declined tile-MMA work prices at the issue rate times its context's fill: true
the filled compute leg is never below the roofline's compute leg: true
a 2x2 register tile's four unrolled accumulators are four chains a thread: true
a one-accumulator reduction is one chain, a 4x4 tile sixteen: true
a serial loop over the cells inside the reduction counts its extent: true
a deeper reduction alone does not change the count: true
a plain leaf's serial batch loop, outside the reduction, is serial work, not chains: true
a global (non-private) accumulator counts no chain: true
four updates of one cell are one chain: true
accumulators whose updates read each other are no independent chains: true
a guarded update counts no chain: true
a repeated target's later update reading another accumulator makes the chains dependent: true
inner loops whose cells may alias prove no chains: true
a looped member beside a single cell of its node may overlap it: no chains: true
elementwise work counts no chain: true
at the cap, the register tile fills a quarter: bitwise its thread-only fill: true
its one-accumulator twin fills a sixteenth: true
the compute legs are each kernel's operations over its fill: 4x the work at 4x the fill: true
so per operation the register-tiled context ranks strictly above its one-accumulator twin: true
one block of 4-chain threads still fills only its share: a sixteenth: true
chains past the cap buy nothing more: true
two chains under the cap of 4 count half a thread each: true
at cap 1 every chain count fills as the thread-only fill: true
unknown chains fill as the thread-only fill at any cap: true
a context at or past the cap fills bitwise as the thread-only fill at any cap: true
the one-accumulator kernel scores bitwise as before at cap 1: true
under the tail, 48 blocks of 64 one-chain threads fill 3/8, of four-chain ones 3/4: true
equal launch shapes of 4 and 1 chains are two contexts, priced each over its own fill: true
the mixed kernel's census splits one launch shape in two (1 and uncounted chains): true
at cap 1 a split launch shape scores bitwise as with no census, where an unmerged sum would not: true
the chains change no operation count: the contexts still sum to the census: true
every scheduled blocktile leaf's reduction carries its register tile's tm*tn chains: true
a 16-chain leaf ranks bitwise as thread-only under a cap of 8: true
a fewer-chain leaf ranks no lower under a cap of 8: true
some under-filled fewer-chain leaf ranks strictly higher under a cap of 8: true
the running backend advises no ILP cap: every leaf ranks bitwise as under the thread-only fill: true
with the fill constants every plain leaf ranks strictly above its bgrid twin: true
without them the twins tie, as before the launch fill: true
every leaf's rank is at least its bound: true
the best-ranked scalar leaf is a bgrid one: true
every launch-sweep geometry's scheduled reduction carries 16 chains a thread: true
